{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21978","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21978","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21978","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang","familyName":"Phan","name":"Phan, Hoang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianjun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xianjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanshun","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yuanshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Bi","name":"Bi, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaocheng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xiaocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madian","familyName":"Khabsa","name":"Khabsa, Madian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lijuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deren","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Deren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Reasoning Gains: Mitigating General-Capability Forgetting in Large Reasoning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T19:08:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:09:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-18T16:29:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-19T01:03:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-26T14:42:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-27T02:22:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) has delivered impressive gains in mathematical and multimodal reasoning and has become a standard post-training paradigm for contemporary language and vision-language models. However, the RLVR recipe introduces a significant risk of capability regression, in which models forget foundational skills after prolonged training without employing regularization strategies. We empirically confirm this concern, observing that open-source reasoning models suffer performance degradation on core capabilities such as perception and faithfulness. While imposing regularization terms like KL divergence can help prevent deviation from the base model, these terms are computed on the current task and therefore do not guarantee preservation of broader knowledge. Meanwhile, commonly used experience replay across heterogeneous domains makes it nontrivial to decide how much training emphasis each objective should receive. To address this, we propose RECAP-a replay strategy with dynamic objective reweighting for general knowledge preservation. Our reweighting mechanism adapts online using short-horizon signals of convergence and instability, shifting the post-training focus away from saturated objectives and toward underperforming or volatile ones. Our method is end-to-end and readily applicable to existing RLVR pipelines without training additional models or heavy tuning. Extensive experiments on benchmarks using Qwen2.5-VL-3B and Qwen2.5-VL-7B demonstrate the effectiveness of our method, which not only preserves general capabilities but also improves reasoning by enabling more flexible trade-offs among in-task rewards."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Implementation is released at https://github.com/VietHoang1512/recap"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21978","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:04:25Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:04:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-27T05:04:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22775","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22775","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22775","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjielong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Junjielong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyin","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Boyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinjia","familyName":"He","name":"He, Pinjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable Supervision for Software Agents via Patch Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T17:52:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T11:43:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:43:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While language model agents have advanced software engineering, existing test-based supervision is limiting its scalability on real-world issues. The reason is twofold: (1) high-coverage tests are naturally rare in the wild, and (2) building and running test sandbox is heavy and fragile. To unlock supervision scaling, we propose R4P, a reasoning-based method that provides scaffold-agnostic rewards. R4P uses a group-wise training objective, enabling it to verify multiple patches against each other's modification and gain a dense reward for supervising agents without executing tests or relying on specific agent trajectories. R4P achieves 72.2% Acc. for verifying patches from SWE-bench, competitive with proprietary models. To show the downstream practical utility of R4P, we design and train an execution-free scaffold, Mini-SE, with pure RL via R4P. Mini-SE achieves 26.2% Pass@1, showing a 10.0% improvement over the original Qwen3-32B, and can be further improved to 32.8% with R4P for test-time scaling on patch selection. The stable scaling curves illustrate that though imperfect, R4P can still reliably support downstream tasks at scale."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP'26 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22775","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:19Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:23:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hetian","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Hetian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CityRiSE: Reasoning Urban Socio-Economic Status in Large Vision-Language Models via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T12:56:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:35:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T11:41:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:44:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Urban socio-economic sensing plays a vital role in advancing global sustainable development goals. With the advent of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), new opportunities have emerged to address this challenge by framing it as a multi-modal perception and reasoning task. However, recent studies show that LVLMs still struggle to make accurate and interpretable socio-economic predictions from visual data. To overcome these limitations and fully exploit the potential of LVLMs, we propose CityRiSE, a novel framework for Reasoning urban Socio-Economic status in LVLMs via reinforcement learning (RL). With carefully curated multi-modal dataset and verifiable reward design, our approach guides the LVLM to focus on semantically meaningful visual cues, enabling structured and goal-oriented reasoning for generalist socio-economic status prediction. Experiments demonstrate that CityRiSE, equipped with emergent reasoning, significantly outperforms existing baselines, improving both prediction accuracy and generalization across diverse urban contexts, especially on unseen cities and unseen indicators. This work highlights the promise of combining RL and LVLMs for interpretable and generalist urban socio-economic sensing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM MM 2026, https://github.com/tsinghua-fib-lab/CityRiSE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:11:39Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:11:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:23:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22087","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22087","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22087","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shvetank","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Shvetank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Mazumder","name":"Mazumder, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arya","familyName":"Tschand","name":"Tschand, Arya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Gohil","name":"Gohil, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Yik","name":"Yik, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zishen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica","familyName":"Quaye","name":"Quaye, Jessica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisavet Lydia","familyName":"Alvanaki","name":"Alvanaki, Elisavet Lydia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avinash","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Avinash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandrashis","familyName":"Mazumdar","name":"Mazumdar, Chandrashis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuhin","familyName":"Khare","name":"Khare, Tuhin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Ingare","name":"Ingare, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ikechukwu","familyName":"Uchendu","name":"Uchendu, Ikechukwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radhika","familyName":"Ghosal","name":"Ghosal, Radhika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Tyagi","name":"Tyagi, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea Mattia","familyName":"Garavagno","name":"Garavagno, Andrea Mattia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alice","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Alice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grace","familyName":"Hur","name":"Hur, Grace","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca P.","familyName":"Carloni","name":"Carloni, Luca P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tushar","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Tushar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankita","familyName":"Nayak","name":"Nayak, Ankita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Yazdanbakhsh","name":"Yazdanbakhsh, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay Janapa","familyName":"Reddi","name":"Reddi, Vijay Janapa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QuArch: A Benchmark for Evaluating LLM Reasoning in Computer Architecture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T23:54:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:18:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-21T03:14:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-22T00:21:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-08T05:22:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:21:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The field of computer architecture, which bridges high-level software abstractions and low-level hardware implementations, remains absent from current large language model (LLM) evaluations. To this end, we present QuArch (pronounced 'quark'), the first benchmark designed to facilitate the development and evaluation of LLM knowledge and reasoning capabilities specifically in computer architecture. QuArch v1.0 provides a comprehensive collection of 2,671 expert-validated question-answer (QA) pairs covering various aspects of computer architecture, including processor design, memory systems, and interconnection networks. Our evaluation reveals that while frontier models possess domain-specific knowledge, they struggle with skills that require higher-order thinking in computer architecture. Frontier model accuracies vary widely (from 34% to 73%) on these advanced questions, highlighting persistent gaps in architectural reasoning across analysis, design, and implementation QAs. Furthermore, via fine-tuning we find that QuArch can translate to improved performance on a realistic memory hierarchy design task, resulting in up to 1.99x more area-efficient solutions and up to 40% more viable solutions overall. By holistically assessing fundamental skills, QuArch provides a foundation for building and measuring LLM capabilities that can accelerate innovation in computing systems. The QuArch benchmark and leaderboard are publicly available at: https://quarch.ai/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22087","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:06:58Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:06:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:13:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22868","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22868","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22868","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"Imani","name":"Imani, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing the Unseen: Towards Training-Free Inspection for Wind Turbine Blades Using Knowledge-Augmented Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T23:19:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:12:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-01T15:11:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:37:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Wind turbine blades operate in harsh environments, making timely damage detection essential for preventing failures and optimizing maintenance. Drone-based inspection and deep learning are promising, but typically depend on large, labeled datasets, which limit their ability to detect rare or evolving damage types. To address this, we propose a zero-shot-oriented inspection framework that integrates Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with Vision-Language Models (VLM). A multimodal knowledge base is constructed, comprising technical documentation, representative reference images, and domain-specific guidelines. A hybrid text-image retriever with keyword-aware reranking assembles the most relevant context to condition the VLM at inference, injecting domain knowledge without task-specific training. We evaluate the framework on 30 labeled blade images covering diverse damage categories. Although the dataset is small due to the difficulty of acquiring verified blade imagery, it covers multiple representative defect types. On this test set, the RAG-grounded VLM correctly classified all samples, whereas the same VLM without retrieval performed worse in both accuracy and precision. We further compare against open-vocabulary baselines and incorporate uncertainty Clopper-Pearson confidence intervals to account for the small-sample setting. Ablation studies indicate that the key advantage of the framework lies in explainability and generalizability: retrieved references ground the reasoning process and enable the detection of previously unseen defects by leveraging domain knowledge rather than relying solely on visual cues. This research contributes a data-efficient solution for industrial inspection that reduces dependence on extensive labeled datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22868","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:29Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:36:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22204","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22204","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22204","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixian","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Weixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Schroder","name":"Schroder, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaohong","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jiaohong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Human-Inspired Neuro-Symbolic World Modeling and Logic Reasoning for Interpretable Safe UAV Landing Site Assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T08:08:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:29:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T09:44:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T02:06:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-17T02:13:37Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v3; The paper has a major update, which is uploaded to https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.02277"},{"date":"2026-07-20T00:14:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliable assessment of safe landing sites in unstructured environments is essential for deploying Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) in real-world applications such as delivery, inspection, and surveillance. Existing learning-based approaches often degrade under covariate shift and offer limited transparency, making their decisions difficult to interpret and validate on resource-constrained platforms. We present NeuroSymLand, a neuro-symbolic framework for marker-free UAV landing site safety assessment that explicitly separates perception-driven world modeling from logic-based safety reasoning. A lightweight segmentation model incrementally constructs a probabilistic semantic scene graph encoding objects, attributes, and spatial relations. Symbolic safety rules, synthesized offline via large language models with human-in-the-loop refinement, are executed directly over this world model at runtime to perform white-box reasoning, producing ranked landing candidates with human-readable explanations of the underlying safety constraints. Across 72 simulated and hardware-in-the-loop landing scenarios, NeuroSymLand achieves 61 successful assessments, outperforming four competitive baselines, which achieve between 37 and 57 successes. Qualitative analysis highlights its superior interpretability and transparent reasoning, while deployment incurs negligible edge overhead. Our results suggest that combining explicit world modeling with symbolic reasoning can support accurate, interpretable, and edge-deployable safety assessment in mobile systems, as demonstrated through UAV landing site assessment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The paper has a major update, which is uploaded to https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.02277"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22204","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:09:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:09:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-20T02:22:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22126","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22126","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22126","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanwen","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Guanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingzehua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jingzehua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubo","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongfang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Dongfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntian","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Juntian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EasyUUV: An LLM-Enhanced Universal and Lightweight Sim-to-Real Reinforcement Learning Framework for UUV Attitude Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T02:55:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:23:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T12:31:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:46:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T18:01:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:06:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite recent advances in Unmanned Underwater Vehicle (UUV) attitude control, existing methods still struggle with generalizability, robustness to real-world disturbances, and efficient deployment. To address the above challenges, this paper presents EasyUUV, a Large Language Model (LLM)-enhanced, platform-agnostic, and lightweight simulation-to-reality reinforcement learning (RL) framework for robust attitude control of UUVs. EasyUUV combines parallelized RL training with a hybrid control architecture, where a learned policy outputs high-level attitude corrections executed by an adaptive S-Surface controller. A multimodal LLM is further integrated to adaptively tune controller parameters at runtime using visual and textual feedback, enabling training-free adaptation to unmodeled dynamics. Also, we have developed a low-cost 6-DoF UUV platform and applied an RL policy trained through efficient parallelized simulation. Extensive simulation and real-world experiments validate the effectiveness and adaptive performance of EasyUUV in achieving robust and adaptive UUV attitude control across diverse underwater conditions. To facilitate reproducibility, the source code, LLM prompts, video, and the supplementary material are provided in the following repositories: Homepage: https://360zmem.github.io/easyuuv/ Video:https://youtu.be/m2yLQzxiIL Supplementary Material: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ImMiyGIPoPyj2ATnXQwbiuOroTSZ2SSs"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22126","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:07:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:07:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:35:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23497","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23497","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23497","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walid","familyName":"Bousselham","name":"Bousselham, Walid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hilde","familyName":"Kuehne","name":"Kuehne, Hilde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cordelia","familyName":"Schmid","name":"Schmid, Cordelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VOLD: Reasoning Transfer from LLMs to Vision-Language Models via On-Policy Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:32:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:54:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T11:09:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:45:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-03T18:11:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:05:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training vision-language models (VLMs) for complex reasoning remains a challenging task, i.a. due to the scarcity of high-quality image-text reasoning data. Conversely, text-based reasoning resources are abundant and scalable, but it is still an open question how to leveraging them for VLM reasoning. To address this problem, we propose VOLD, a framework to transfer reasoning capabilities from text-only teacher models to VLM student models. To this end, VOLD combines reinforcement learning via Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with on-policy distillation, which allows the student reasoning traces to be guided by the teacher model, resulting in a significant gain over using GRPO alone. We further show that a cold-start alignment is essential for an effective transfer during the online training phase in this scenario and that without sufficient distributional alignment between teacher and student, on-policy distillation fails to provide meaningful guidance. We evaluate VOLD across diverse benchmarks including MMMU-Pro, MathVision, MathVista, and LogicVista, showing that VOLD outperforms the baseline model significantly and improves over the state of the art by a margin. Our ablation shows the importance of a cold-start alignment via SFT for on-policy distillation with a text-only teacher."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"www.walidbousselham.com/VOLD/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23497","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:29Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:04:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23008","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23008","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23008","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuli","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qiuli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhuang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xinhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonglin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yonglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingpeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xingpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Prompt Optimization to Multi-Dimensional Credibility Evaluation: Enhancing Trustworthiness of Chinese LLM-Generated Liver MRI Reports -- with Preliminary Extension to Lung Cancer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T04:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:22:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T02:12:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:24:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T08:27:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:35:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated promising performance in generating diagnostic conclusions from imaging findings, thereby supporting radiology reporting, trainee education, and quality control. However, systematic guidance on how to optimize prompt design across different clinical contexts remains underexplored. Moreover, a comprehensive and standardized framework for assessing the trustworthiness of LLM-generated radiology reports is yet to be established. This study aims to enhance the trustworthiness of LLM-generated liver MRI reports by introducing a Multi-Dimensional Credibility Assessment (MDCA) framework and providing guidance on institution-specific prompt optimization. The proposed framework is applied to evaluate and compare the performance of several advanced LLMs, including Kimi-K2-Instruct-0905, Qwen3-235B-A22B-Instruct-2507, DeepSeek-V3, and ByteDance-Seed-OSS-36B-Instruct, using the SiliconFlow platform."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 6 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23008","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:28:49Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:28:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:05:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21828","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21828","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21828","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingbing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Lingbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huajun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Huajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structured and Abstractive Reasoning on Multi-modal Relational Knowledge Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T02:23:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T06:52:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:28:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding and reasoning with abstractive information from the visual modality presents significant challenges for current multi-modal large language models (MLLMs). Among the various forms of abstractive information, Multi-Modal Relational Knowledge (MMRK), which represents abstract relational structures between multi-modal entities using node-edge formats, remains largely under-explored. In particular, STructured and Abstractive Reasoning (STAR) on such data has received little attention from the research community. To bridge the dual gaps in large-scale high-quality data and capability enhancement methodologies, this paper makes the following key contributions: (i). An automatic STAR data engine capable of synthesizing images with MMRK to build multi-modal instruction data with reliable chain-of-thought thinking for various STAR tasks and (ii). A comprehsive two-stage capability enhancement training framework, accompanied by a suite of evaluation protocols tailored to different STAR tasks. Based upon these contributions, we introduce STAR-64K, a dataset comprising 64K high-quality multi-modal instruction samples, and conduct experiments across 5 open-source MLLMs. Experimental results show that our two-stage enhancement framework enables smaller 3B/7B models to significantly outperform GPT-4o in STAR. Additionally, we provide in-depth analysis regarding the effectiveness of various designs, data transferability, and scalability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Findings of ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21828","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:20:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22977","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22977","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22977","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenlong","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Chenlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyang","familyName":"Sha","name":"Sha, Zeyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwen","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Shiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhua","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Changhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zechao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zechao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Reasoning Trap: How Enhancing LLM Reasoning Amplifies Tool Hallucination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:58:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:20:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-17T17:15:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T01:01:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Enhancing the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) is a key strategy for building Agents that \"think then act.\" However, recent observations, like OpenAI's o3, suggest a paradox: stronger reasoning often coincides with increased hallucination, yet no prior work has systematically examined whether reasoning enhancement itself causes tool hallucination. To address this gap, we pose the central question: Does strengthening reasoning increase tool hallucination? To answer this, we introduce SimpleToolHalluBench, a diagnostic benchmark measuring tool hallucination in two failure modes: (i) no tool available, and (ii) only distractor tools available. Through controlled experiments, we establish three key findings. First, we demonstrate a causal relationship: progressively enhancing reasoning through RL increases tool hallucination proportionally with task performance gains. Second, this effect transcends overfitting - training on non-tool tasks (e.g., mathematics) still amplifies subsequent tool hallucination. Third, the effect is method-agnostic, appearing when reasoning is instilled via supervised fine-tuning and when it is merely elicited at inference by switching from direct answers to step-by-step thinking. We also evaluate mitigation strategies including Prompt Engineering and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), revealing a fundamental reliability-capability trade-off: reducing hallucination consistently degrades utility. Mechanistically, Reasoning RL disproportionately collapses tool-reliability-related representations, and hallucinations surface as amplified divergences concentrated in late-layer residual streams. These findings reveal that current reasoning enhancement methods inherently amplify tool hallucination, highlighting the need for new training objectives that jointly optimize for capability and reliability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACL 2026 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22977","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:28:04Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:28:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:09:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21977","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21977","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21977","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengfei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distribution Shift Alignment Helps LLMs Simulate Survey Response Distributions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T19:04:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:09:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-17T12:40:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) offer a promising way to simulate human survey responses, potentially reducing the cost of large-scale data collection. However, existing zero-shot methods suffer from prompt sensitivity and low accuracy, while conventional fine-tuning approaches mostly fit the training set distributions and struggle to produce results more accurate than the training set itself, which deviates from the original goal of using LLMs to simulate survey responses. Building on this observation, we introduce Distribution Shift Alignment (DSA), a two-stage fine-tuning method that aligns both the output distributions and the distribution shifts across different backgrounds. By learning how these distributions change rather than fitting training data, DSA can provide results substantially closer to the true distribution than the training data. Empirically, DSA consistently outperforms other methods on five public survey datasets. We further conduct a comprehensive comparison covering accuracy, robustness, and data savings. DSA reduces the required real data by 53.48-69.12%, demonstrating its effectiveness and efficiency in survey simulation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21977","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:04:23Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:04:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:09:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22543","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22543","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22543","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Haibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FAPO: Flawed-Aware Policy Optimization for Efficient and Reliable Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T05:49:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:52:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T08:46:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:33:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) has emerged as a promising paradigm for enhancing the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). In this context, models explore reasoning trajectories and exploit rollouts with correct answers as positive signals for policy optimization. However, these rollouts might involve flawed patterns such as answer-guessing and jump-in-reasoning. Such flawed-positive rollouts are rewarded identically to fully correct ones, causing policy models to internalize these unreliable reasoning patterns. In this work, we first conduct a systematic study of flawed-positive rollouts in RL and find that they enable rapid capability gains during the early optimization stage, while constraining reasoning capability later by reinforcing unreliable patterns. Building on these insights, we propose Flawed-Aware Policy Optimization (FAPO), which presents a parameter-free reward penalty for flawed-positive rollouts, enabling the policy to leverage them as useful shortcuts in the warm-up stage, securing stable early gains, while gradually shifting optimization toward reliable reasoning in the later refinement stage. To accurately and comprehensively detect flawed-positive rollouts, we introduce a generative reward model (GenRM) with a process-level reward that precisely localizes reasoning errors. Experiments show that FAPO is effective in broad domains, improving outcome correctness, process reliability, and training stability without increasing the token budget."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026. Project page: https://fapo-rl.github.io/; Infra Doc: https://verl.readthedocs.io/en/latest/advance/reward_loop.html"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22543","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:17:49Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:17:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:07:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jiayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carl","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Carl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hongkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incentivizing Agentic Reasoning in LLM Judges via Tool-Integrated Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T06:03:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:23:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-21T05:25:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:16:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are widely used as judges to evaluate response quality, providing a scalable alternative to human evaluation. However, most LLM judges operate solely on intrinsic text-based reasoning, limiting their ability to verify complex constraints or perform accurate computation. Motivated by the success of tool-integrated reasoning (TIR) in numerous tasks, we propose TIR-Judge, an end-to-end RL framework for training LLM judges that integrates a code executor for precise evaluation. TIR-Judge is built on three principles: (i) diverse training across verifiable and non-verifiable domains, (ii) flexible judgment formats (pointwise, pairwise, listwise), and (iii) iterative RL that bootstraps directly from the initial model without distillation. On seven public benchmarks, TIR-Judge surpasses strong reasoning-based judges by up to 6.4% (pointwise) and 7.7% (pairwise), and achieves listwise performance comparable to Claude-Opus-4 despite having only 8B parameters. Remarkably, TIR-Judge-Zero - trained entirely without distilled judge trajectories, matches the performance of distilled variants, demonstrating that tool-augmented judges can self-evolve through iterative reinforcement learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:29:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:29:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:01:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22389","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22389","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22389","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike","familyName":"Thelwall","name":"Thelwall, Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Mohammadi","name":"Mohammadi, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Small and Reasoning Large Language Models Score Journal Articles for Research Quality and Do Averaging and Few-shot Help?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T18:12:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:41:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-17T13:09:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-18T01:38:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Previous research has shown that journal article quality ratings from the cloud based Large Language Model (LLM) families ChatGPT and Gemini and the medium sized open weights LLM Gemma3 27b correlate moderately with expert research quality scores. This article assesses whether other medium sized LLMs, smaller LLMs, and reasoning models have similar abilities. This is tested with Gemma3 variants, Llama4 Scout, Qwen3, Magistral Small and DeepSeek R1 on a dataset of 2,780 medical, health and life science papers in 6 fields, with two different gold standards, one novel. Few-shot and score averaging approaches are also evaluated. The results suggest that medium-sized LLMs have similar performance to ChatGPT 4o-mini and Gemini 2.0 Flash, but that 1b parameters may often, and 4b sometimes, be too few. Reasoning models did not have a clear advantage. Moreover, averaging scores from multiple identical queries seems to be a universally successful strategy, and there is weak evidence that few-shot prompts (four examples) tend to help. Overall, the results show, for the first time, that smaller LLMs \u0026gt;4b have a substantial capability to rate journal articles for research quality, especially if score averaging is used, but that reasoning does not give an advantage for this task; it is therefore not recommended because it is slow. The use of LLMs to support research evaluation is now more credible since multiple variants have a similar ability, including many that can be deployed offline in a secure environment without substantial computing resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Thelwall, M. \u0026amp; Mohammadi, E. (2026). Can small and reasoning Large Language Models score journal articles for research quality and do averaging and few-shot help? Scientometrics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22389","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:14:13Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:14:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-18T02:57:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23596","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23596","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23596","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhu","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Yizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julien Veron","familyName":"Vialard","name":"Vialard, Julien Veron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleksii","familyName":"Kuchaiev","name":"Kuchaiev, Oleksii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Delalleau","name":"Delalleau, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Think Twice: Branch-and-Rethink Reasoning Reward Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:58:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:59:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-18T07:32:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T01:29:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-29T18:57:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-30T02:20:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly rely on thinking models that externalize intermediate steps and allocate extra test-time compute, with think-twice strategies showing that a deliberate second pass can elicit stronger reasoning. In contrast, most reward models (RMs) still compress many quality dimensions into a single scalar in one shot, a design that induces judgment diffusion: attention spreads across evaluation criteria, yielding diluted focus and shallow analysis. We introduce branch-and-rethink (BR-RM), a two-turn RM that transfers the think-twice principle to reward modeling. Turn 1 performs adaptive branching, selecting a small set of instance-critical dimensions (such as factuality and safety) and sketching concise, evidence-seeking hypotheses. Turn 2 executes branch-conditioned rethinking, a targeted reread that tests those hypotheses and scrutinizes only what matters most. We train with GRPO-style reinforcement learning over structured two-turn traces using a simple binary outcome reward with strict format checks, making the approach compatible with standard RLHF pipelines. By converting all-at-once scoring into focused, second-look reasoning, BR-RM reduces judgment diffusion and improves sensitivity to subtle yet consequential errors while remaining practical and scalable. Experimental results demonstrate that our model achieves state-of-the-art performance on three challenging reward modeling benchmarks across diverse domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Source Code: https://github.com/yzjiao/BR-RM. Model Checkpoints: https://huggingface.co/nvidia/Qwen3-Nemotron-14B-BRRM and https://huggingface.co/nvidia/Qwen3-Nemotron-8B-BRRM"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23596","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:53Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:42:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22922","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22922","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22922","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runtao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Runtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giang","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Giang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Kharya","name":"Kharya, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anh Totti","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Anh Totti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chirag","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Chirag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Human Verification of LLM Reasoning through Interactive Explanation Interfaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T01:57:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:16:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T18:39:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:05:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-02T22:06:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:07:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-26T05:44:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:10:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) have led to their increasing employment in several critical applications, particularly education, where they support problem-solving, tutoring, and personalized study. Chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning capabilities [1, 2] are well-known to help LLMs decompose a problem into steps and explore the solution spaces more effectively, leading to impressive performance on mathematical and reasoning benchmarks. As the length of CoT tokens per question increases substantially to even thousands of tokens per question [ 1], it is unknown how users could comprehend LLM reasoning and detect errors or hallucinations. To address this problem and understand how reasoning can improve human-AI interaction, we present three new interactive reasoning interfaces: interactive CoT (iCoT), interactive Program-of-Thought (iPoT), and interactive Graph (iGraph). That is, we ask LLMs themselves to generate an interactive web interface wrapped around the original CoT content, which may be presented in text (iCoT), graphs (iGraph) or code (iPoT). This interface allows users to interact with and provide a novel experience in reading and validating the reasoning chains of LLMs. Across a study of 125 participants, interactive interfaces significantly improve user performance. Specifically, iGraph users score the highest error detection rate (85.6%), followed by iPoT (82.5%), iCoT (80.6%), all outperforming standard CoT (73.5%). Interactive interfaces also lead to faster user validation time-iGraph users are faster (57.9 secs per question) than the users of iCoT and iPoT (60 secs) and the standard CoT (64.7 secs). A post-study questionnaire shows that users prefer iGraph, citing its superior ability to enable them to follow the LLM's reasoning. We discuss the implications of these results and provide recommendations for the future design of reasoning models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22922","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:26:45Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:26:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:37:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linyang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Linyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianjun","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Tianjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Antonello","name":"Antonello, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gavin","familyName":"Mischler","name":"Mischler, Gavin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Micah","familyName":"Goldblum","name":"Goldblum, Micah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nima","familyName":"Mesgarani","name":"Mesgarani, Nima","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Far from the Shallow: Brain-Predictive Reasoning Embedding through Residual Disentanglement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T22:46:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:12:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-20T00:43:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:11:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-24T09:48:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:24:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding how the human brain progresses from processing simple linguistic inputs to performing high-level reasoning is a fundamental challenge in neuroscience. While modern large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used to model neural responses to language, their internal representations are highly \"entangled,\" mixing information about lexicon, syntax, meaning, and reasoning. This entanglement biases conventional brain encoding analyses toward linguistically shallow features (e.g., lexicon and syntax), making it difficult to isolate the neural substrates of cognitively deeper processes. Here, we introduce a residual disentanglement method that computationally isolates these components. By first probing an LM to identify feature-specific layers, our method iteratively regresses out lower-level representations to produce four nearly orthogonal embeddings for lexicon, syntax, meaning, and, critically, reasoning. We used these disentangled embeddings to model intracranial (ECoG) brain recordings from neurosurgical patients listening to natural speech. We show that: 1) This isolated reasoning embedding exhibits unique predictive power, accounting for variance in neural activity not explained by other linguistic features and even extending to the recruitment of visual regions beyond classical language areas. 2) The neural signature for reasoning is temporally distinct, peaking later (~350-400ms) than signals related to lexicon, syntax, and meaning, consistent with its position atop a processing hierarchy. 3) Standard, non-disentangled LLM embeddings can be misleading, as their predictive success is primarily attributable to linguistically shallow features, masking the more subtle contributions of deeper cognitive processing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:18Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:37:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22881","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22881","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22881","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saeed","familyName":"Najafi","name":"Najafi, Saeed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alona","familyName":"Fyshe","name":"Fyshe, Alona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Offline Preference Optimization via Maximum Marginal Likelihood Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T00:15:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:13:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T20:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:06:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning Large Language Models (LLMs) with human preferences is crucial, but standard methods like Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) are often complex and unstable. In this work, we propose a new, simpler approach that recasts alignment through the lens of Maximum Marginal Likelihood (MML) estimation. Our new MML based Preference Optimization (MMPO) maximizes the marginal log-likelihood of a preferred text output, using the preference pair as samples for approximation, and forgoes the need for both an explicit reward model and entropy maximization. We theoretically demonstrate that MMPO implicitly performs preference optimization, producing a weighted gradient that naturally up-weights chosen responses over rejected ones. Across models ranging from 135M to 8B parameters, we empirically show that MMPO: 1) is more stable with respect to the hyperparameter $β$ compared to alternative baselines, and 2) achieves competitive or superior preference alignment while better preserving the base model's general language capabilities. Through a series of ablation experiments, we show that this improved performance is indeed attributable to MMPO's implicit preference optimization within the gradient updates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EACL 2026, camera ready"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22881","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:37:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23027","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23027","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23027","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaohan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjie","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaru","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Yaru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zewen","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Zewen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifang","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Zhifang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furu","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Furu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Stable and Effective Reinforcement Learning for Mixture-of-Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T05:47:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:23:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T15:14:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:28:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in reinforcement learning (RL) have substantially improved the training of large-scale language models, leading to significant gains in generation quality and reasoning ability. However, most existing research focuses on dense models, while RL training for Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures remains underexplored. To address the instability commonly observed in MoE training, we propose a novel router-aware approach to optimize importance sampling (IS) weights in off-policy RL. Specifically, we design a rescaling strategy guided by router logits, which effectively reduces gradient variance and mitigates training divergence. Experimental results demonstrate that our method significantly improves both the convergence stability and the final performance of MoE models, highlighting the potential of RL algorithmic innovations tailored to MoE architectures and providing a promising direction for efficient training of large-scale expert models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Added additional experiments, improved analysis, and fixed minor issues"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23027","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:29:16Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:29:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:27:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22836","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22836","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22836","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanyu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Guanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiucheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qiucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaowen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Handong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Handong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking the Text-Vision Reasoning Imbalance in MLLMs through the Lens of Training Recipes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T21:06:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:10:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T22:24:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:09:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities on vision-and-language tasks. However, recent findings reveal an imbalance in their reasoning capabilities across visual and textual modalities. Specifically, current MLLMs often over-rely on textual cues while under-attending to visual content, resulting in suboptimal performance on tasks that require genuine visual reasoning. We refer to this phenomenon as the \\textit{modality gap}, defined as the performance disparity between text-centric and vision-centric inputs. In this paper, we analyze the modality gap through the lens of training recipes. We first show that existing training recipes tend to amplify this gap. Then, we systematically explore strategies to bridge it from two complementary perspectives: data and loss design. Our findings provide insights into developing training recipes that mitigate the modality gap and promote more balanced multimodal reasoning. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/UCSB-NLP-Chang/Bridging-Modality-Gap."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22836","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:24:44Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:24:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-09T03:11:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeongseop","familyName":"Rha","name":"Rha, Hyeongseop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeong Hun","familyName":"Yeo","name":"Yeo, Jeong Hun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeonju","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yeonju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong Man","familyName":"Ro","name":"Ro, Yong Man","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emotion-Coherent Reasoning for Multimodal LLMs via Emotional Rationale Verifier"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:40:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:54:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-12T04:50:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:23:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T15:47:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:59:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-05T13:24:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-06T02:15:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The recent advancement of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) is transforming human-computer interaction (HCI) from surface-level exchanges into more nuanced and emotionally intelligent communication. To realize this shift, emotion understanding becomes essential allowing systems to capture subtle cues underlying user intent. Furthermore, providing faithful explanations for predicted emotions is crucial to ensure interpretability and build user trust. However, current MLLM-based methods often generate emotion explanations that diverge from the target labels and sometimes even contradict their own predicted emotions. This inconsistency poses a critical risk for misunderstanding and erodes reliability in interactive settings. To address this, we propose a novel approach: the Emotional Rationale Verifier (ERV) and an Explanation Reward. Our method guides the model to produce reasoning that is explicitly consistent with the target emotion during multimodal emotion recognition without modifying the model architecture or requiring additional paired video-description annotations. Our method significantly improves faithful explanation-prediction consistency and explanation emotion accuracy on the MAFW and DFEW datasets. Through extensive experiments and human evaluations, we show that our approach not only enhances alignment between explanation and prediction but also empowers MLLMs to deliver emotionally coherent, trustworthy interactions, marking a key step toward truly human-like HCI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:43Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:11:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22693","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22693","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22693","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenlong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Rao","name":"Rao, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuiguang","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Shuiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VADTree: Explainable Training-Free Video Anomaly Detection via Hierarchical Granularity-Aware Tree"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T14:36:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:03:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T16:57:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T01:04:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-30T12:31:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-01T01:27:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video anomaly detection (VAD) focuses on identifying anomalies in videos. Supervised methods demand substantial in-domain training data and fail to deliver clear explanations for anomalies. In contrast, training-free methods leverage the knowledge reserves and language interactivity of large pre-trained models to detect anomalies. However, the current fixed-length temporal window sampling approaches struggle to accurately capture anomalies with varying temporal spans. Therefore, we propose VADTree that utilizes a Hierarchical Granularityaware Tree (HGTree) structure for flexible sampling in VAD. VADTree leverages the knowledge embedded in a pre-trained Generic Event Boundary Detection (GEBD) model to characterize potential anomaly event boundaries. Specifically, VADTree decomposes the video into generic event nodes based on boundary confidence, and performs adaptive coarse-fine hierarchical structuring and redundancy removal to construct the HGTree. Then, the multi-dimensional priors are injected into the visual language models (VLMs) to enhance the node-wise anomaly perception, and anomaly reasoning for generic event nodes is achieved via large language models (LLMs). Finally, an inter-cluster node correlation method is used to integrate the multi-granularity anomaly scores. Extensive experiments on three challenging datasets demonstrate that VADTree achieves state-of-the-art performance in training-free settings while drastically reducing the number of sampled video segments. The code will be available at https://github.com/wenlongli10/VADTree."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 poster"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22693","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:21:23Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:21:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-01T03:39:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22888","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22888","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22888","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihao","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Shihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongming","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Chongming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianshan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jianshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiming","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Ruiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuli","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Fuli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MGFRec: Towards Reinforced Reasoning Recommendation with Multiple Groundings and Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T00:41:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:14:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T09:10:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:26:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The powerful reasoning and generative capabilities of large language models (LLMs) have inspired researchers to apply them to reasoning-based recommendation tasks, which require in-depth reasoning about user interests and the generation of recommended items. However, previous reasoning-based recommendation methods have typically performed inference within the language space alone, without incorporating the actual item space. This has led to over-interpreting user interests and deviating from real items. Towards this research gap, we propose performing multiple rounds of grounding during inference to help the LLM better understand the actual item space, which could ensure that its reasoning remains aligned with real items. Furthermore, we introduce a user agent that provides feedback during each grounding step, enabling the LLM to better recognize and adapt to user interests. Comprehensive experiments conducted on three Amazon review datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of incorporating multiple groundings and feedback. These findings underscore the critical importance of reasoning within the actual item space, rather than being confined to the language space, for recommendation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at KDD 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22888","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:57Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:51:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22340","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22340","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22340","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changti","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Changti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijie","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Shijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laurence Tianruo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Laurence Tianruo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DynaSolidGeo: A Dynamic Benchmark for Genuine Spatial Mathematical Reasoning of VLMs in Solid Geometry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T15:49:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:39:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-11T15:16:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-12T01:54:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Solid geometry problem solving demands spatial mathematical reasoning that integrates spatial intelligence and symbolic reasoning. However, most existing multimodal mathematical reasoning benchmarks focus primarily on 2D plane geometry, rely on static datasets prone to data contamination and memorization, and evaluate models solely by final answers, overlooking the reasoning process. To address these limitations, we introduce DynaSolidGeo, the first dynamic benchmark for evaluating genuine spatial reasoning in Vision-Language Models (VLMs). Constructed through a semi-automatic annotation pipeline, DynaSolidGeo contains 503 expert-curated seed questions that can, in principle, dynamically generate an unbounded number of diverse multimodal text-visual instances. Beyond answer accuracy, we incorporate process evaluation based on expert-annotated reasoning chains to measure logical validity and causal coherence. Experiments across representative open-source and closed-source VLMs reveal large performance gaps, severe degradation in dynamic settings, and poor performance on tasks requiring high-level spatial intelligence, such as mental rotation and visualization. The code and dataset are available at \\href{https://zgca-ai4edu.github.io/DynaSolidGeo/}{DynaSolidGeo}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The code and dataset are available at \\href{https://zgca-ai4edu.github.io/DynaSolidGeo/}{DynaSolidGeo}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22340","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:03Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-12T03:31:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21861","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21861","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21861","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Course Correct Labs, Independent Research Group"],"givenName":"Bentley","familyName":"DeVilling","name":"DeVilling, Bentley","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Mirror Loop: Recursive Non-Convergence in Generative Reasoning Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T07:53:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:04:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-05T09:41:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:34:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are often described as capable of reflective reasoning, yet recursive self-evaluation without external feedback frequently yields reformulation rather than progress. We test this prediction in a cross-provider study of 144 reasoning sequences across three models (OpenAI GPT-4o-mini, Anthropic Claude 3 Haiku, and Google Gemini 2.0 Flash) and four task families (arithmetic, code, explanation, reflection), each iterated ten times under two conditions: ungrounded self-critique and a minimal grounding intervention (a single verification step at iteration three). Mean informational change (delta I, measured via normalized edit distance) declined by 55% from early (0.193) to late (0.087) iterations in ungrounded runs, with consistent patterns across all three providers. Grounded runs showed a +28% rebound in informational change immediately after the intervention and sustained non-zero variance thereafter. Complementary measures-n-gram novelty, embedding drift, and character-level entropy-converged on the same pattern: reflection without contact tends toward informational closure. We interpret this as evidence for a structural limit on self-correction in generative reasoning: without an exchange of information with an independent verifier or environment, recursive inference approaches an attractor state of epistemic stasis. Minimal grounding functions as dissipative coupling, reintroducing informational flux. The cross-architecture consistency suggests the mirror loop arises from shared autoregressive training objectives rather than provider-specific alignment schemes. The results delineate when reflection is performative rather than epistemic and motivate design principles for grounded, cooperative reasoning. Materials and code are publicly available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 2 figures. Category: cs.LG. Code and data: https://github.com/Course-Correct-Labs/mirror-loop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21861","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:41Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:16:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23081","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23081","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23081","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengying","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Chengying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuemiao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuemiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongxiang","familyName":"Weng","name":"Weng, Rongxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rumei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Rumei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfei","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Hongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunliang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Xunliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey on LLM Mid-Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T07:32:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:27:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T11:00:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:39:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in foundation models have highlighted the significant benefits of multi-stage training, with a particular emphasis on the emergence of mid-training as a vital stage that bridges pre-training and post-training. Mid-training is distinguished by its use of intermediate data and computational resources, systematically enhancing specified capabilities such as mathematics, coding, reasoning, and long-context extension, while maintaining foundational competencies. This survey provides a formal definition of mid-training for large language models (LLMs) and investigates optimization frameworks that encompass data curation, training strategies, and model architecture optimization. We analyze mainstream model implementations in the context of objective-driven interventions, illustrating how mid-training serves as a distinct and critical stage in the progressive development of LLM capabilities. By clarifying the unique contributions of mid-training, this survey offers a comprehensive taxonomy and actionable insights, supporting future research and innovation in the advancement of LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23081","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:30:34Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:30:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-05T03:13:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23590","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23590","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23590","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheol Woo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Cheol Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shresth","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Shresth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauricio","familyName":"Tec","name":"Tec, Mauricio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milind","familyName":"Tambe","name":"Tambe, Milind","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:55:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T15:33:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) has become a popular method for fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) due to its stability and simplicity. However, it is also known to be sensitive to noise in the data and prone to overfitting. Recent works have proposed using distributionally robust optimization (DRO) to address potential noise and distributional shift in the data. However, these methods often suffer from excessive conservatism and high computational cost. We propose DPO-PRO (DPO with Preference Robustness), a robust fine-tuning algorithm based on DPO which accounts for uncertainty in the preference distribution through a lightweight DRO formulation. Unlike prior DRO-based variants, DPO-PRO focuses solely on uncertainty in preferences, avoiding unnecessary conservatism and incurring negligible computational overhead. We further show that DPO-PRO is equivalent to a regularized DPO objective that penalizes model overconfidence under weak preference signals. We evaluate DPO-PRO on standard alignment benchmarks and a real-world public health task. Experimental results show that our method consistently improves robustness to noisy preference signals compared to existing DPO variants."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"arXiv admin note: substantial text overlap with arXiv:2509.02709"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23590","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:44Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:26:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22118","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22118","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22118","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karim","familyName":"Elmaaroufi","name":"Elmaaroufi, Karim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liheng","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Liheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Svegliato","name":"Svegliato, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjit A.","familyName":"Seshia","name":"Seshia, Sanjit A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matei","familyName":"Zaharia","name":"Zaharia, Matei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRAID: Enhancing Spatial Reasoning of VLMs Through High-Fidelity Data Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T02:07:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:22:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:53:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:19:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Language Models (VLMs) achieve strong performance on many vision-language tasks but often struggle with spatial reasoning$\\unicode{x2014}$a prerequisite for many applications. Empirically, we find that a dataset produced by a current training data generation pipeline has a 57.6% human validation rate. These rates stem from current limitations: single-image 3D reconstruction introduces cascading modeling errors and requires wide answer tolerances, while caption-based methods require hyper-detailed annotations and suffer from generative hallucinations. We present GRAID, built on the key insight that qualitative spatial relationships can be reliably determined from 2D geometric primitives alone. By operating exclusively on 2D bounding boxes from standard object detectors, GRAID avoids both 3D reconstruction errors and generative hallucinations, resulting in datasets that are of higher quality than existing tools that produce similar datasets as validated by human evaluations. We apply our framework to the BDD100k, NuImages, and Waymo datasets, generating over 8.5 million high-quality VQA pairs creating questions spanning spatial relations, counting, ranking, and size comparisons. We evaluate one of the datasets and find it achieves 91.16% human-validated accuracy$\\unicode{x2014}$compared to 57.6% on a dataset generated by recent work. Critically, we demonstrate that when trained on GRAID data, models learn spatial reasoning concepts that generalize: models fine-tuned on 6 question types improve on over 10 held-out types, with accuracy gains of 47.5% on BDD and 37.9% on NuImages for Llama 3.2B 11B, and when trained on all questions types, achieve improvements on several existing benchmarks such as BLINK. The GRAID framework, datasets, and additional information can be found $\\href{this https URL}{here}$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables, project page: https://ke7.github.io/graid/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22118","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:07:43Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:07:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:25:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23569","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23569","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23569","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoqi","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Baoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jilan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jilan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuping","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yuping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangmiao","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Jiangmiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EgoThinker: Unveiling Egocentric Reasoning with Spatio-Temporal CoT"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:38:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:58:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Egocentric video reasoning centers on an unobservable agent behind the camera who dynamically shapes the environment, requiring inference of hidden intentions and recognition of fine-grained interactions. This core challenge limits current multimodal large language models MLLMs, which excel at visible event reasoning but lack embodied, first-person understanding. To bridge this gap, we introduce EgoThinker, a novel framework that endows MLLMs with robust egocentric reasoning capabilities through spatio-temporal chain-of-thought supervision and a two-stage learning curriculum. First, we introduce EgoRe-5M, a large-scale egocentric QA dataset constructed from 13M diverse egocentric video clips. This dataset features multi-minute segments annotated with detailed CoT rationales and dense hand-object grounding. Second, we employ SFT on EgoRe-5M to instill reasoning skills, followed by reinforcement fine-tuning RFT to further enhance spatio-temporal localization. Experimental results show that EgoThinker outperforms existing methods across multiple egocentric benchmarks, while achieving substantial improvements in fine-grained spatio-temporal localization tasks. Full code and data are released at https://github.com/InternRobotics/EgoThinker."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23569","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:15Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:42:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23532","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23532","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23532","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirban","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Anirban","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irtaza","familyName":"Khalid","name":"Khalid, Irtaza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Peñaloza","name":"Peñaloza, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Schockaert","name":"Schockaert, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When No Paths Lead to Rome: Benchmarking Systematic Neural Relational Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:09:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:56:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Designing models that can learn to reason in a systematic way is an important and long-standing challenge. In recent years, a wide range of solutions have been proposed for the specific case of systematic relational reasoning, including Neuro-Symbolic approaches, variants of the Transformer architecture, and specialised Graph Neural Networks. However, existing benchmarks for systematic relational reasoning focus on an overly simplified setting, based on the assumption that reasoning can be reduced to composing relational paths. In fact, this assumption is hard-baked into the architecture of several recent models, leading to approaches that can perform well on existing benchmarks but are difficult to generalise to other settings. To support further progress in the field of systematic relational reasoning with neural networks, we introduce NoRA, a new benchmark which adds several levels of difficulty and requires models to go beyond path-based reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted at NeurIPS 2025 D\u0026amp;B track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23532","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:41:20Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:41:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:41:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23473","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23473","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23473","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingjian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xingjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linxin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Linxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runhao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Runhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hecheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongyuan","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Zongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelian","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xuelian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video-Thinker: Sparking \"Thinking with Videos\" via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:10:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:52:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in image reasoning methods, particularly \"Thinking with Images\", have demonstrated remarkable success in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs); however, this dynamic reasoning paradigm has not yet been extended to video reasoning tasks. In this paper, we propose Video-Thinker, which empowers MLLMs to think with videos by autonomously leveraging their intrinsic \"grounding\" and \"captioning\" capabilities to generate reasoning clues throughout the inference process. To spark this capability, we construct Video-Thinker-10K, a curated dataset featuring autonomous tool usage within chain-of-thought reasoning sequences. Our training strategy begins with Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) to learn the reasoning format, followed by Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to strengthen this reasoning capability. Through this approach, Video-Thinker enables MLLMs to autonomously navigate grounding and captioning tasks for video reasoning, eliminating the need for constructing and calling external tools. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Video-Thinker achieves significant performance gains on both in-domain tasks and challenging out-of-domain video reasoning benchmarks, including Video-Holmes, CG-Bench-Reasoning, and VRBench. Our Video-Thinker-7B substantially outperforms existing baselines such as Video-R1 and establishes state-of-the-art performance among 7B-sized MLLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23473","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:39:55Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:39:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:39:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23397","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23397","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23397","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen-Hua","familyName":"Ling","name":"Ling, Zhen-Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Limin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Limin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yali","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VideoTG-R1: Boosting Video Temporal Grounding via Curriculum Reinforcement Learning on Reflected Boundary Annotations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T14:55:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:48:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video temporal grounding (VTG) aims to locate precise segments in videos based on language queries, which is a fundamental challenge in video understanding. While recent Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have shown promise in tackling VTG through reinforcement learning (RL), they overlook the challenges arising from both the quality and difficulty of training samples. (1) Partially annotated samples. Many samples contain relevant segments beyond the annotated interval, introducing ambiguous supervision. (2) Hard-to-ground samples. Samples with poor zero-shot performance produce consistently low and indistinguishable rewards during RL training, exhibiting no clear preference among multiple outputs and thus hindering learning efficiency. To address these challenges, we propose VideoTG-R1, a novel curriculum RL framework with reflected boundary annotations, enabling data-efficient training. Specifically, we propose a Boundary Reflection Agent that utilizes MLLMs to predict query-relevant timestamps outside the annotated intervals, allowing us to identify and filter out partially annotated samples, thereby reducing ambiguity. Furthermore, we introduce a Difficulty Estimation Agent to assess the training difficulty of each sample and design a curriculum RL strategy that dynamically masks the videos of hard-to-ground samples according to the training steps, easing the training difficulty and providing clearer preference. Experiments on the VTG and grounded VideoQA tasks demonstrate the effectiveness of our method. Remarkably, with only 10% of the training samples and 21% of the computational budget, VideoTG-R1 outperforms full-data counterparts under both group relative policy optimization (GRPO) and supervised fine-tuning (SFT). The code is available at https://github.com/ldong1111/VideoTG-R1."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23397","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:38:06Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:38:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:38:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23393","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23393","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23393","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farid","familyName":"Bagirov","name":"Bagirov, Farid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikhail","familyName":"Arkhipov","name":"Arkhipov, Mikhail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ksenia","familyName":"Sycheva","name":"Sycheva, Ksenia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evgeniy","familyName":"Glukhov","name":"Glukhov, Evgeniy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Egor","familyName":"Bogomolov","name":"Bogomolov, Egor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Best of N Worlds: Aligning Reinforcement Learning with Best-of-N Sampling via max@k Optimisation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T14:47:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:47:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The application of Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) to mathematical and coding domains has demonstrated significant improvements in the reasoning and problem-solving abilities of Large Language Models. Despite its success in single generation problem solving, the reinforcement learning fine-tuning process may harm the model's exploration ability, as reflected in decreased diversity of generations and a resulting degradation of performance during Best-of-N sampling for large N values. In this work, we focus on optimizing the max@k metric, a continuous generalization of pass@k. We derive an unbiased on-policy gradient estimate for direct optimization of this metric. Furthermore, we extend our derivations to the off-policy updates, a common element in modern RLVR algorithms, that allows better sample efficiency. Empirically, we show that our objective effectively optimizes max@k metric in off-policy scenarios, aligning the model with the Best-of-N inference strategy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23393","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:38:01Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:38:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:38:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23337","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23337","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23337","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pai","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Pai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jizheng","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Sihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BaZi-Based Character Simulation Benchmark: Evaluating AI on Temporal and Persona Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T13:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:44:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human-like virtual characters are crucial for games, storytelling, and virtual reality, yet current methods rely heavily on annotated data or handcrafted persona prompts, making it difficult to scale up and generate realistic, contextually coherent personas. We create the first QA dataset for BaZi-based persona reasoning, where real human experiences categorized into wealth, health, kinship, career, and relationships are represented as life-event questions and answers. Furthermore, we propose the first BaZi-LLM system that integrates symbolic reasoning with large language models to generate temporally dynamic and fine-grained virtual personas. Compared with mainstream LLMs such as DeepSeek-v3 and GPT-5-mini, our method achieves a 30.3%-62.6% accuracy improvement. In addition, when incorrect BaZi information is used, our model's accuracy drops by 20%-45%, showing the potential of culturally grounded symbolic-LLM integration for realistic character simulation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23337","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:36:41Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:36:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:36:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23264","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23264","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23264","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanyi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Huanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijie","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Lijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PAHQ: Accelerating Automated Circuit Discovery through Mixed-Precision Inference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T12:24:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:40:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Circuit discovery, which involves identifying sparse and task-relevant subnetworks in pre-trained language models, is a cornerstone of mechanistic interpretability. Automated Circuit Discovery (ACDC) has emerged as a pivotal methodology in circuit discovery, but its application to large language models is severely limited by computational inefficiency and prohibitively high memory requirements. Although several accelerated approaches have been proposed, they primarily rely on linear approximations to ACDC, which significantly compromises analytical faithfulness. Our proposed method for accelerating automated circuit discovery, Per Attention Head Quantization (PAHQ), takes a fundamentally different approach by optimizing the efficiency of each individual patching operation. PAHQ leverages a fundamental alignment between activation patching and mixed-precision quantization (MPQ): interpretability analysis through patching essentially performs targeted ablation studies. Therefore, we can maintain high precision exclusively for investigated components while safely reducing precision elsewhere in the network. PAHQ-accelerated ACDC reduces runtime by up to 80\\% and memory consumption by up to 30\\% compared to unaccelerated ACDC while maintaining faithfulness. Importantly, our method readily integrates with existing edge-based circuit discovery techniques by modifying the attention computation mechanism. This training-free approach provides a practical and novel pathway for accelerating mechanistic interpretability methods. Our code is available at https://github.com/626619403/PAHQ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23264","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:34:57Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:34:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:34:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre P","familyName":"Uchoa","name":"Uchoa, Alexandre P","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlo E T","familyName":"Oliveira","name":"Oliveira, Carlo E T","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudia L R","familyName":"Motta","name":"Motta, Claudia L R","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Stakeholder Alignment in LLM-Powered Collaborative AI Systems: A Multi-Agent Framework for Intelligent Tutoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T12:06:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:39:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The integration of Large Language Models into Intelligent Tutoring Systems pre-sents significant challenges in aligning with diverse and often conflicting values from students, parents, teachers, and institutions. Existing architectures lack for-mal mechanisms for negotiating these multi-stakeholder tensions, creating risks in accountability and bias. This paper introduces the Advisory Governance Layer (AGL), a non-intrusive, multi-agent framework designed to enable distributed stakeholder participation in AI governance. The AGL employs specialized agents representing stakeholder groups to evaluate pedagogical actions against their spe-cific policies in a privacy-preserving manner, anticipating future advances in per-sonal assistant technology that will enhance stakeholder value expression. Through a novel policy taxonomy and conflict-resolution protocols, the frame-work provides structured, auditable governance advice to the ITS without altering its core pedagogical decision-making. This work contributes a reference architec-ture and technical specifications for aligning educational AI with multi-stakeholder values, bridging the gap between high-level ethical principles and practical implementation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:34:30Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:34:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:34:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23148","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23148","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23148","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aryan","familyName":"Mathur","name":"Mathur, Aryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asaduddin","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Asaduddin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adapting Interleaved Encoders with PPO for Language-Guided Reinforcement Learning in BabyAI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.9; I.5.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T09:24:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:32:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep reinforcement learning agents often struggle when tasks require understanding both vision and language. Conventional architectures typically isolate perception (for example, CNN-based visual encoders) from decision-making (policy networks). This separation can be inefficient, since the policy's failures do not directly help the perception module learn what is important. To address this, we implement the Perception-Decision Interleaving Transformer (PDiT) architecture introduced by Mao et al. (2023), a model that alternates between perception and decision layers within a single transformer. This interleaving allows feedback from decision-making to refine perceptual features dynamically. In addition, we integrate a contrastive loss inspired by CLIP to align textual mission embeddings with visual scene features. We evaluate the PDiT encoders on the BabyAI GoToLocal environment and find that the approach achieves more stable rewards and stronger alignment compared to a standard PPO baseline. The results suggest that interleaved transformer encoders are a promising direction for developing more integrated autonomous agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Undergraduate research project, IIT Palakkad, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23148","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:32:11Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:32:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:32:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junguang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Junguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziru","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ziru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiancan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiancan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Think before Recommendation: Autonomous Reasoning-enhanced Recommender"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T07:26:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:26:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The core task of recommender systems is to learn user preferences from historical user-item interactions. With the rapid development of large language models (LLMs), recent research has explored leveraging the reasoning capabilities of LLMs to enhance rating prediction tasks. However, existing distillation-based methods suffer from limitations such as the teacher model's insufficient recommendation capability, costly and static supervision, and superficial transfer of reasoning ability. To address these issues, this paper proposes RecZero, a reinforcement learning (RL)-based recommendation paradigm that abandons the traditional multi-model and multi-stage distillation approach. Instead, RecZero trains a single LLM through pure RL to autonomously develop reasoning capabilities for rating prediction. RecZero consists of two key components: (1) \"Think-before-Recommendation\" prompt construction, which employs a structured reasoning template to guide the model in step-wise analysis of user interests, item features, and user-item compatibility; and (2) rule-based reward modeling, which adopts group relative policy optimization (GRPO) to compute rewards for reasoning trajectories and optimize the LLM. Additionally, the paper explores a hybrid paradigm, RecOne, which combines supervised fine-tuning with RL, initializing the model with cold-start reasoning samples and further optimizing it with RL. Experimental results demonstrate that RecZero and RecOne significantly outperform existing baseline methods on multiple benchmark datasets, validating the superiority of the RL paradigm in achieving autonomous reasoning-enhanced recommender systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 poster"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:30:28Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:30:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:30:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22909","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22909","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22909","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongshun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zongshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Matta","name":"Matta, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Inference Placement for Deep Learning across Edge and Cloud Platforms: A Multi-Objective Optimization Perspective and Future Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T01:26:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:15:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Edge intelligent applications like VR/AR and language model based chatbots have become widespread with the rapid expansion of IoT and mobile devices. However, constrained edge devices often cannot serve the increasingly large and complex deep learning (DL) models. To mitigate these challenges, researchers have proposed optimizing and offloading partitions of DL models among user devices, edge servers, and the cloud. In this setting, users can take advantage of different services to support their intelligent applications. For example, edge resources offer low response latency. In contrast, cloud platforms provide low monetary cost computation resources for computation-intensive workloads. However, communication between DL model partitions can introduce transmission bottlenecks and pose risks of data leakage. Recent research aims to balance accuracy, computation delay, transmission delay, and privacy concerns. They address these issues with model compression, model distillation, transmission compression, and model architecture adaptations, including internal classifiers. This survey contextualizes the state-of-the-art model offloading methods and model adaptation techniques by studying their implication to a multi-objective optimization comprising inference latency, data privacy, and resource monetary cost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22909","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:26:27Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:26:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:26:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22892","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22892","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22892","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingzehua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jingzehua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangfei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yangfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanwen","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Guanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Never Too Rigid to Reach: Adaptive Virtual Model Control with LLM- and Lyapunov-Based Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T00:46:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:14:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic arms are increasingly deployed in uncertain environments, yet conventional control pipelines often become rigid and brittle when exposed to perturbations or incomplete information. Virtual Model Control (VMC) enables compliant behaviors by embedding virtual forces and mapping them into joint torques, but its reliance on fixed parameters and limited coordination among virtual components constrains adaptability and may undermine stability as task objectives evolve. To address these limitations, we propose Adaptive VMC with Large Language Model (LLM)- and Lyapunov-Based Reinforcement Learning (RL), which preserves the physical interpretability of VMC while supporting stability-guaranteed online adaptation. The LLM provides structured priors and high-level reasoning that enhance coordination among virtual components, improve sample efficiency, and facilitate flexible adjustment to varying task requirements. Complementarily, Lyapunov-based RL enforces theoretical stability constraints, ensuring safe and reliable adaptation under uncertainty. Extensive simulations on a 7-DoF Panda arm demonstrate that our approach effectively balances competing objectives in dynamic tasks, achieving superior performance while highlighting the synergistic benefits of LLM guidance and Lyapunov-constrained adaptation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22892","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:26:03Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:26:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:26:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22878","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22878","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas I-Hsien","familyName":"Kuo","name":"Kuo, Nicholas I-Hsien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Blanca","familyName":"Gallego","name":"Gallego, Blanca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louisa","familyName":"Jorm","name":"Jorm, Louisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Limits of Generative Pre-Training in Structured EMR Trajectories with Irregular Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T00:04:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:13:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models refer to architectures trained on vast datasets using autoregressive pre-training from natural language processing to capture intricate patterns and motifs. They were originally developed to transfer such learned knowledge to downstream predictive tasks. Recently, however, some studies repurpose these learned representations for phenotype discovery without rigorous validation, risking superficially realistic but clinically incoherent embeddings. To test this mismatch, we trained two autoregressive models -- a sequence-to-sequence LSTM and a reduced Transformer -- on longitudinal ART for HIV and Acute Hypotension datasets. Controlled irregularity was added during training via random inter-visit gaps, while test sequences stayed complete. Patient-trajectory synthesis evaluated distributional and correlational fidelity. Both reproduced feature distributions but failed to preserve cross-feature structure -- showing that generative pre-training yields local realism but limited clinical coherence. These results highlight the need for domain-specific evaluation and support trajectory synthesis as a practical probe before fine-tuning or deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:43Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:25:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianne","familyName":"Arriola","name":"Arriola, Marianne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yair","familyName":"Schiff","name":"Schiff, Yair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Phung","name":"Phung, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Gokaslan","name":"Gokaslan, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Volodymyr","familyName":"Kuleshov","name":"Kuleshov, Volodymyr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Encoder-Decoder Diffusion Language Models for Efficient Training and Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T22:05:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:11:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Discrete diffusion models enable parallel token sampling for faster inference than autoregressive approaches. However, prior diffusion models use a decoder-only architecture, which requires sampling algorithms that invoke the full network at every denoising step and incur high computational cost. Our key insight is that discrete diffusion models perform two types of computation: 1) representing clean tokens and 2) denoising corrupted tokens, which enables us to use separate modules for each task. We propose an encoder-decoder architecture to accelerate discrete diffusion inference, which relies on an encoder to represent clean tokens and a lightweight decoder to iteratively refine a noised sequence. We also show that this architecture enables faster training of block diffusion models, which partition sequences into blocks for better quality and are commonly used in diffusion language model inference. We introduce a framework for Efficient Encoder-Decoder Diffusion (E2D2), consisting of an architecture with specialized training and sampling algorithms, and we show that E2D2 achieves superior trade-offs between generation quality and inference throughput on summarization, translation, and mathematical reasoning tasks. We provide the code, model weights, and blog post on the project page: https://m-arriola.com/e2d2"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025. We provide the code at https://github.com/kuleshov-group/e2d2"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22849","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22849","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22849","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Stein","name":"Stein, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neelay","familyName":"Velingker","name":"Velingker, Neelay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayur","familyName":"Naik","name":"Naik, Mayur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Once Upon an Input: Reasoning via Per-Instance Program Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T21:58:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:11:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) excel at zero-shot inference but continue to struggle with complex, multi-step reasoning. Recent methods that augment LLMs with intermediate reasoning steps such as Chain of Thought (CoT) and Program of Thought (PoT) improve performance but often produce undesirable solutions, especially in algorithmic domains. We introduce Per-Instance Program Synthesis (PIPS), a method that generates and refines programs at the instance-level using structural feedback without relying on task-specific guidance or explicit test cases. To further improve performance, PIPS incorporates a confidence metric that dynamically chooses between direct inference and program synthesis on a per-instance basis. Experiments across three frontier LLMs and 30 benchmarks including all tasks of Big Bench Extra Hard (BBEH), visual question answering tasks, relational reasoning tasks, and mathematical reasoning tasks show that PIPS improves the absolute harmonic mean accuracy by up to 8.6% and 9.4% compared to PoT and CoT respectively, and reduces undesirable program generations by 65.1% on the algorithmic tasks compared to PoT with Gemini-2.0-Flash."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025. 34 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22849","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:03Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:25:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aya","familyName":"Nakayama","name":"Nakayama, Aya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuji","familyName":"Nishimura","name":"Nishimura, Yuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaito","familyName":"Tanaka","name":"Tanaka, Kaito","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic-Preserving Cross-Style Visual Reasoning for Robust Multi-Modal Understanding in Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T21:11:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:10:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The \"style trap\" poses a significant challenge for Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), hindering robust semantic understanding across diverse visual styles, especially in in-context learning (ICL). Existing methods often fail to effectively decouple style from content, hindering generalization. To address this, we propose the Semantic-Preserving Cross-Style Visual Reasoner (SP-CSVR), a novel framework for stable semantic understanding and adaptive cross-style visual reasoning. SP-CSVR integrates a Cross-Style Feature Encoder (CSFE) for style-content disentanglement, a Semantic-Aligned In-Context Decoder (SAICD) for efficient few-shot style adaptation, and an Adaptive Semantic Consistency Module (ASCM) employing multi-task contrastive learning to enforce cross-style semantic invariance. Extensive experiments on a challenging multi-style dataset demonstrate SP-CSVR's state-of-the-art performance across visual captioning, visual question answering, and in-context style adaptation. Comprehensive evaluations, including ablation studies and generalization analysis, confirm SP-CSVR's efficacy in enhancing robustness, generalization, and efficiency across diverse visual styles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:24:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:24:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:24:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22594","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22594","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22594","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingqing","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Bingqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songtao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Songtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyi","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Mingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Framework for Quantifying How Pre-Training and Context Benefit In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T09:21:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:56:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained large language models have demonstrated a strong ability to learn from context, known as in-context learning (ICL). Despite a surge of recent applications that leverage such capabilities, it is by no means clear, at least theoretically, how the ICL capabilities arise, and in particular, what is the precise role played by key factors such as pre-training procedure as well as context construction. In this work, we propose a new framework to analyze the ICL performance, for a class of realistic settings, which includes network architectures, data encoding, data generation, and prompt construction process. As a first step, we construct a simple example with a one-layer transformer, and show an interesting result, namely when the pre-train data distribution is different from the query task distribution, a properly constructed context can shift the output distribution towards the query task distribution, in a quantifiable manner, leading to accurate prediction on the query topic. We then extend the findings in the previous step to a more general case, and derive the precise relationship between ICL performance, context length and the KL divergence between pre-train and query task distribution. Finally, we provide experiments to validate our theoretical results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22594","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:19:01Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:19:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:19:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitong","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Kaitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijia","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keze","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Keze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Backward-Friendly Optimization: Training Large Language Models with Approximate Gradients under Memory Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T00:50:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:46:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Full fine-tuning of Large Language Models (LLMs) is notoriously memory-intensive, primarily because conventional optimizers such as SGD or Adam assume access to exact gradients derived from cached activations. Existing solutions either alter the model architecture (e.g., reversible networks) or trade memory for computation (e.g., activation checkpointing), but the optimizer itself remains untouched. In this work, we introduce GradLite, a backward-friendly optimizer that relaxes the requirement of exact gradients, enabling efficient training even when intermediate activations are aggressively discarded or approximated. GradLite leverages two key techniques: (i) low-rank Jacobian approximation, which reduces the dimensionality of backpropagated error signals, and (ii) error-feedback correction, which accumulates and compensates approximation errors across iterations to preserve convergence guarantees. We provide a theoretical analysis showing that GradLite maintains unbiased gradient estimates with bounded variance, ensuring convergence rates comparable to Adam. Empirically, GradLite reduces optimizer-state and activation memory consumption by up to 50\\% without architectural changes, and achieves on-par or superior downstream performance on reasoning (MMLU, GSM8K), multilingual, and dialogue benchmarks compared to checkpointing and optimizer-centric baselines (LoMo, GaLore)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:16:04Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:16:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:16:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Revanth","familyName":"Rameshkumar","name":"Rameshkumar, Revanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jimson","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jimson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunxin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yunxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abulhair","familyName":"Saparov","name":"Saparov, Abulhair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning Models Reason Well, Until They Don't"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T17:28:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:40:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown significant progress in reasoning tasks. However, recent studies show that transformers and LLMs fail catastrophically once reasoning problems exceed modest complexity. We revisit these findings through the lens of large reasoning models (LRMs) -- LLMs fine-tuned with incentives for step-by-step argumentation and self-verification. LRM performance on graph and reasoning benchmarks such as NLGraph seem extraordinary, with some even claiming they are capable of generalized reasoning and innovation in reasoning-intensive fields such as mathematics, physics, medicine, and law. However, by more carefully scaling the complexity of reasoning problems, we show existing benchmarks actually have limited complexity. We develop a new dataset, the Deep Reasoning Dataset (DeepRD), along with a generative process for producing unlimited examples of scalable complexity. We use this dataset to evaluate model performance on graph connectivity and natural language proof planning. We find that the performance of LRMs drop abruptly at sufficient complexity and do not generalize. We also relate our LRM results to the distributions of the complexities of large, real-world knowledge graphs, interaction graphs, and proof datasets. We find the majority of real-world examples fall inside the LRMs' success regime, yet the long tails expose substantial failure potential. Our analysis highlights the near-term utility of LRMs while underscoring the need for new methods that generalize beyond the complexity of examples in the training distribution."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:48Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Ahmad Hosseini","familyName":"Miangoleh","name":"Miangoleh, Seyed Ahmad Hosseini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin Jalal","familyName":"Aghdasian","name":"Aghdasian, Amin Jalal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farzaneh","familyName":"Abdollahi","name":"Abdollahi, Farzaneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BLIP-FusePPO: A Vision-Language Deep Reinforcement Learning Framework for Lane Keeping in Autonomous Vehicles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T17:27:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:40:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we propose Bootstrapped Language-Image Pretraining-driven Fused State Representation in Proximal Policy Optimization (BLIP-FusePPO), a novel multimodal reinforcement learning (RL) framework for autonomous lane-keeping (LK), in which semantic embeddings generated by a vision-language model (VLM) are directly fused with geometric states, LiDAR observations, and Proportional-Integral-Derivative-based (PID) control feedback within the agent observation space. The proposed method lets the agent learn driving rules that are aware of their surroundings and easy to understand by combining high-level scene understanding from the VLM with low-level control and spatial signals. Our architecture brings together semantic, geometric, and control-aware representations to make policy learning more robust. A hybrid reward function that includes semantic alignment, LK accuracy, obstacle avoidance, and speed regulation helps learning to be more efficient and generalizable. Our method is different from the approaches that only use semantic models to shape rewards. Instead, it directly embeds semantic features into the state representation. This cuts down on expensive runtime inference and makes sure that semantic guidance is always available. The simulation results show that the proposed model is better at LK stability and adaptability than the best vision-based and multimodal RL baselines in a wide range of difficult driving situations. We make our code publicly available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://github.com/Amin-A96/BLIP-FusePPO-A-Vision-Language-Deep-Reinforcement-Learning-Framework-for-Lane-Keeping-in-Autonomous.git"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:46Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiazheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Damianou","name":"Damianou, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J","familyName":"Rosser","name":"Rosser, J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"José Luis Redondo","familyName":"García","name":"García, José Luis Redondo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantina","familyName":"Palla","name":"Palla, Konstantina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mapping Faithful Reasoning in Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T16:48:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:40:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chain-of-thought (CoT) traces promise transparency for reasoning language models, but prior work shows they are not always faithful reflections of internal computation. This raises challenges for oversight: practitioners may misinterpret decorative reasoning as genuine. We introduce Concept Walk, a general framework for tracing how a model's internal stance evolves with respect to a concept direction during reasoning. Unlike surface text, Concept Walk operates in activation space, projecting each reasoning step onto the concept direction learned from contrastive data. This allows us to observe whether reasoning traces shape outcomes or are discarded. As a case study, we apply Concept Walk to the domain of Safety using Qwen 3-4B. We find that in 'easy' cases, perturbed CoTs are quickly ignored, indicating decorative reasoning, whereas in 'hard' cases, perturbations induce sustained shifts in internal activations, consistent with faithful reasoning. The contribution is methodological: Concept Walk provides a lens to re-examine faithfulness through concept-specific internal dynamics, helping identify when reasoning traces can be trusted and when they risk misleading practitioners."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, Accepted to the Mechanistic Interpretability Workshop at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:35Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22192","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22192","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22192","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiongwei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xiongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Kuang","name":"Kuang, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianye","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Jianye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OptiTree: Hierarchical Thoughts Generation with Tree Search for LLM Optimization Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T07:19:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:28:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optimization modeling is one of the most crucial but technical parts of operations research (OR). To automate the modeling process, existing works have leveraged large language models (LLMs), prompting them to break down tasks into steps for generating variables, constraints, and objectives. However, due to the highly complex mathematical structures inherent in OR problems, standard fixed-step decomposition often fails to achieve high performance. To address this challenge, we introduce OptiTree, a novel tree search approach designed to enhance modeling capabilities for complex problems through adaptive problem decomposition into simpler subproblems. Specifically, we develop a modeling tree that organizes a wide range of OR problems based on their hierarchical problem taxonomy and complexity, with each node representing a problem category and containing relevant high-level modeling thoughts. Given a problem to model, we recurrently search the tree to identify a series of simpler subproblems and synthesize the global modeling thoughts by adaptively integrating the hierarchical thoughts. Experiments show that OptiTree significantly improves the modeling accuracy compared to the state-of-the-art, achieving over 10\\% improvements on the challenging benchmarks. The code is released at https://github.com/MIRALab-USTC/OptiTree/tree/main."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22192","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:09:30Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:09:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:09:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22132","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22132","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22132","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuying","familyName":"LI","name":"LI, Xuying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Controllable Mathematical Reasoning via Self-Optimizing Thought Vectors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T03:13:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:23:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a novel approach for controllable mathematical reasoning that leverages self-optimizing thought vectors with entropy minimization. Our method introduces learnable thought vectors that dynamically modulate the internal reasoning process of large language models. Using Gemma-2-9B on GSM8K, we achieve 90.1% accuracy with a controllability score of 0.42, demonstrating that entropy-based rewards effectively guide focused reasoning patterns without requiring external reward annotations. Our analysis reveals distinct thought vector clusters and consistent low-entropy distributions across control conditions, validating our framework for controllable AI reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22132","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:08:03Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:08:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:08:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22084","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22084","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22084","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atij","familyName":"Mahesh","name":"Mahesh, Atij","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compositional Bias Control in Large Language Models: Preference Learning Fails, Supervision Succeeds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T23:52:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:18:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) still produce gender-stereotyped language even in occupation-neutral contexts that reflect deep societal biases (Rudinger et al., 2018). To address this, prior work has proposed prompting, constrained decoding (Dathathri et al., 2020; Zhou et al., 2024), post-processing, and fine-tuning-based alignment (Rafailov et al., 2023; Ravfogel et al., 2022). However, the comparative efficacy and learning dynamics remain little understood. We report a comparative analysis of six control techniques for bias mitigation: prompt-only, generate-and-filter, DFA-based Ctrl-G decoding, Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), and Iterative Nullspace Projection (INLP). We evaluate each method on a compositional constraint task. This task requires generating sentences that contain at least one agentic and one communal descriptor for each of the twenty Winogender-derived occupations. We quantify trade-offs between control strength and naturalness with evaluations of constraint compliance, lexical diversity, and fluency. Our results reveal key contrasts among the methods: SFT achieves 99.87 +- 0.15% compliance and high lexical diversity, while DPO, despite similar training stability, fails at 4.53 +- 0.82%. Ctrl-G guarantees perfect compliance, but at the cost of severely reduced fluency and diversity. Preference-based learning fundamentally differs: it cannot satisfy compositional constraints, as binary preference signals encode ranking, not logical conjunctions. Only explicit positive supervision enables mitigation of compositional biases; preference-based alignment fails to generalize logical structures, underscoring the limitations of preference learning and the necessity of explicit supervision for fair and fluent controlled generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22084","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:06:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:06:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:06:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22034","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22034","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22034","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rick","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Rick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Ternasky","name":"Ternasky, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron Ontoyin","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Aaron Ontoyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianling","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Xianling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuat","familyName":"Alican","name":"Alican, Fuat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yigit","familyName":"Ihlamur","name":"Ihlamur, Yigit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-AR: LLM-powered Automated Reasoning Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T21:36:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:13:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) can already identify patterns and reason effectively, yet their variable accuracy hampers adoption in high-stakes decision-making applications. In this paper, we study this issue from a venture capital perspective by predicting idea-stage startup success based on founder traits. (i) To build a reliable prediction model, we introduce LLM-AR, a pipeline inspired by neural-symbolic systems that distils LLM-generated heuristics into probabilistic rules executed by the ProbLog automated-reasoning engine. (ii) An iterative policy-evolution loop incorporates association-rule mining to progressively refine the prediction rules.\n On unseen folds, LLM-AR achieves 59.5% precision and 8.7% recall, 5.9x the random baseline precision, while exposing every decision path for human inspection. The framework is interpretable and tunable via hyperparameters, showing promise to extend into other domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22034","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:05:44Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:05:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:05:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21906","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21906","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21906","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang Hao","familyName":"Cheong","name":"Cheong, Kang Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structure-Aware Cooperative Ensemble Evolutionary Optimization on Combinatorial Problems with Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evolutionary algorithms (EAs) have proven effective in exploring the vast solution spaces typical of graph-structured combinatorial problems. However, traditional encoding schemes, such as binary or numerical representations, often fail to straightforwardly capture the intricate structural properties of networks. Through employing the image-based encoding to preserve topological context, this study utilizes multimodal large language models (MLLMs) as evolutionary operators to facilitate structure-aware optimization over graph data. To address the visual clutter inherent in large-scale network visualizations, we leverage graph sparsification techniques to simplify structures while maintaining essential structural features. To further improve robustness and mitigate bias from different sparsification views, we propose a cooperative evolutionary optimization framework that facilitates cross-domain knowledge transfer and unifies multiple sparsified variants of diverse structures. Additionally, recognizing the sensitivity of MLLMs to network layout, we introduce an ensemble strategy that aggregates outputs from various layout configurations through consensus voting. Finally, experiments on real-world networks through various tasks demonstrate that our approach improves both the quality and reliability of solutions in MLLM-driven evolutionary optimization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21906","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:02:44Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:02:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:02:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21881","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21881","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21881","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nannan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Nannan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanyu","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Chuanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shipeng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Shipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Man","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Man","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoThought: A Dataset for Enhancing Mathematical Geometry Reasoning in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T16:43:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:04:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong reasoning capabilities in text-based mathematical problem solving; however, when adapted to visual reasoning tasks, particularly geometric problem solving, their performance substantially declines because geometric problems present unique challenges. Specifically, these challenges stem from two key factors: first, the intrinsic complexity of geometry requiring detailed image comprehension and multi-step reasoning, and second, the limitations of existing datasets which lack sufficient scale, diversity, and explicit reasoning traces, consequently hindering effective model training. To address these challenges, we developed the GeoThoughts dataset, a comprehensive geometric reasoning corpus with two subsets: Geo-Thought-6K with 6,243 samples and its augmented version Geo-Thought-Augmented-10K containing 10,834 samples. Each entry includes visual descriptions, step-by-step solutions, explicit reasoning chains, reflection steps, and final answers. Using this dataset, we developed GeoThought-MLLM, a mathematical reasoning multimodal model that generates detailed thinking processes during problem-solving. Our model outperforms existing benchmarks in geometric tasks, demonstrating that training with our Chain-of-Thought dataset improves geometric reasoning capabilities across both in-domain and out-of-domain settings. Finally, we analyze failure cases and observe that errors primarily arise from incorrect interpretation of mathematical concepts or spatial misjudgment. By invoking CoT to correct these mistakes, the model produces correct answers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21881","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:02:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:02:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:02:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanqing","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Chuanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Chenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengda","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhengda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LSF-Animation: Label-Free Speech-Driven Facial Animation via Implicit Feature Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T10:09:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:04:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech-driven 3D facial animation has attracted increasing interest since its potential to generate expressive and temporally synchronized digital humans. While recent works have begun to explore emotion-aware animation, they still depend on explicit one-hot encodings to represent identity and emotion with given emotion and identity labels, which limits their ability to generalize to unseen speakers. Moreover, the emotional cues inherently present in speech are often neglected, limiting the naturalness and adaptability of generated animations. In this work, we propose LSF-Animation, a novel framework that eliminates the reliance on explicit emotion and identity feature representations. Specifically, LSF-Animation implicitly extracts emotion information from speech and captures the identity features from a neutral facial mesh, enabling improved generalization to unseen speakers and emotional states without requiring manual labels. Furthermore, we introduce a Hierarchical Interaction Fusion Block (HIFB), which employs a fusion token to integrate dual transformer features and more effectively integrate emotional, motion-related and identity-related cues. Extensive experiments conducted on the 3DMEAD dataset demonstrate that our method surpasses recent state-of-the-art approaches in terms of emotional expressiveness, identity generalization, and animation realism. The source code will be released at: https://github.com/Dogter521/LSF-Animation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:45Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:01:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21853","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21853","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21853","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debdeep","familyName":"Sanyal","name":"Sanyal, Debdeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aakash Sen","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Aakash Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhruv","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Dhruv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurabh","familyName":"Deshpande","name":"Deshpande, Saurabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murari","familyName":"Mandal","name":"Mandal, Murari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Policy Optimization Prefers The Path of Least Resistance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T21:48:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Policy optimization (PO) algorithms are used to refine Large Language Models for complex, multi-step reasoning. Current state-of-the-art pipelines enforce a strict think-then-answer format to elicit chain-of-thought (CoT); however, the behavior of PO when these rigid constraints are relaxed into an open-ended CoT structure remains an under-studied question. We investigate this gap with an extensive suite of controlled experiments and identify a consistent principle: \\textit{policy optimization consistently follows the path of least resistance}. When afforded the flexibility to interleave reasoning and response, policy optimization consistently learns to discard explicit reasoning, causing the policy to degenerate to a direct \\texttt{}-only format. This outcome holds true across various models and algorithms. We find that this collapse in format is persistent even when the complex \\texttt{} format is assigned up to 4x larger reward weights. We formalize this principle through a series of controlled reward decomposition experiments, demonstrating a clear hierarchy: PO systematically optimizes for the simplest reward component first, a preference that holds even when faced with mutually exclusive choices or strong incentives for more complex behaviors. Finally, we show that successful convergence on the high-reward shortcut is not a low-effort drift but is driven by the optimization process that requires the KL-regularized policy to have sufficient freedom to make a significant shift from its initial prior. Our findings reveal that granting policies the freedom to diverge is a double-edged sword: while necessary for discovering high-reward shortcuts, it also creates a powerful incentive to game the simplest aspects of the reward function, posing a critical challenge for reward hacking under alignment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 8 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21853","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:30Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:01:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21847","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21847","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21847","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kaiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junchao","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Junchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenlong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanli","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Wanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SynCast: Synergizing Contradictions in Precipitation Nowcasting via Diffusion Sequential Preference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T16:11:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Precipitation nowcasting based on radar echoes plays a crucial role in monitoring extreme weather and supporting disaster prevention. Although deep learning approaches have achieved significant progress, they still face notable limitations. For example, deterministic models tend to produce over-smoothed predictions, which struggle to capture extreme events and fine-scale precipitation patterns. Probabilistic generative models, due to their inherent randomness, often show fluctuating performance across different metrics and rarely achieve consistently optimal results. Furthermore, precipitation nowcasting is typically evaluated using multiple metrics, some of which are inherently conflicting. For instance, there is often a trade-off between the Critical Success Index (CSI) and the False Alarm Ratio (FAR), making it challenging for existing models to deliver forecasts that perform well on both metrics simultaneously. To address these challenges, we introduce preference optimization into precipitation nowcasting for the first time, motivated by the success of reinforcement learning from human feedback in large language models. Specifically, we propose SynCast, a method that employs the two-stage post-training framework of Diffusion Sequential Preference Optimization (Diffusion-SPO), to progressively align conflicting metrics and consistently achieve superior performance. In the first stage, the framework focuses on reducing FAR, training the model to effectively suppress false alarms. Building on this foundation, the second stage further optimizes CSI with constraints that preserve FAR alignment, thereby achieving synergistic improvements across these conflicting metrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21847","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:21Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:01:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21807","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21807","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21807","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiaao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shenwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjie","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Mingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzheng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Wenzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghao","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Chenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Man","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Man","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Activating Visual Context and Commonsense Reasoning through Masked Prediction in VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-21T08:50:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:02:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent breakthroughs in reasoning models have markedly advanced the reasoning capabilities of large language models, particularly via training on tasks with verifiable rewards. Yet, a significant gap persists in their adaptation to real world multimodal scenarios, most notably, vision language tasks, due to a heavy focus on single modal language settings. While efforts to transplant reinforcement learning techniques from NLP to VLMs have emerged, these approaches often remain confined to perception centric tasks or reduce images to textual summaries, failing to fully exploit visual context and commonsense knowledge, ultimately constraining the generalization of reasoning capabilities across diverse multimodal environments. To address this limitation, we introduce a novel fine tuning task, Masked Prediction via Context and Commonsense, which forces models to integrate visual context and commonsense reasoning by reconstructing semantically meaningful content from occluded images, thereby laying the foundation for generalized reasoning. To systematically evaluate the model performance in generalized reasoning, we developed a specialized evaluation benchmark, MPCC Eval, and employed various fine tuning strategies to guide reasoning. Among these, we introduced an innovative training method, Reinforcement Fine tuning with Prior Sampling, which not only enhances model performance but also improves its generalized reasoning capabilities in OOD and cross task scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21807","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:25Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:00:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kejia","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kejia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiacong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kewei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Kewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zunlei","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingli","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Mingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Token-Level Inference-Time Alignment for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-20T09:58:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:02:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have become essential backbones of modern multimodal intelligence, yet their outputs remain prone to hallucination-plausible text misaligned with visual inputs. Existing alignment approaches often rely on expensive fine-tuning with annotated preference data or sequence-level inference strategies that provide only coarse, delayed feedback. To overcome these limitations, we present TITA (Token-level Inference-Time Alignment), a lightweight framework that freezes the base VLM and instead trains a reward model to approximate its distribution. During inference, implicit preference signals are extracted as log-probability ratios between the reward model and the target VLM, yielding dense autoregressive feedback. This formulation can be viewed as an inference-time variant of Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), providing token-level corrective signals without retraining the backbone. Extensive evaluations on LLaVA-1.5-7B and 13B show consistent gains across 12 benchmarks, with improvements of 8.6% on MMVet and 6.7% on POPE, indicating stronger general understanding and reduced hallucinations. Additional experiments on Qwen2.5-VL-7B and DeepSeek-VL2-27.5B show comparable gains, especially in hallucination reduction and VQA accuracy, while incurring negligible inference overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:05Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:00:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":61,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-27T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-28T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+transformer%29+AND+titles.title%3A%28training+OR+optimization+OR+alignment+OR+reinforcement+OR+preference+OR+distillation+OR+reasoning+OR+representation%29%29\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}